  13: { id: 'qc-13', type: 'sequencing', title: 'Step by Step', instructions: 'Put the events from Chapter 13 in the right order.', question: 'How did the opposition to Islam grow step by step?', sequencingItems: [{ id: '1', text: 'The leaders laughed whenever the Prophet (pbuh) walked by.' }, { id: '2', text: 'The Qur’an criticized the idols and warned against false worship.' }, { id: '3', text: 'Umayya ordered Bilal to give up his religion.' }, { id: '4', text: 'The leaders began a social and economic boycott against Muslims.' }], correctAnswer: ['1', '2', '3', '4'], explanation: '“At first, the leaders of Mecca only mocked Islam.” “But when the Qur’an criticized the idols and warned against false worship, their opposition became violent,” and poor Muslims such as Bilal suffered the most. Then “the leaders of Mecca went further”: between 617 and 620 they began a boycott.', feedback: { correct: 'Well done. The opposition grew from mocking, to violence against weak Muslims, to a boycott.', incorrect: 'Look for the step words in Chapter 13: “At first …”, “But when …”, and “The leaders of Mecca went further.”' } },
